$internal
$recursive
$no.symbols
$no.xref

subroutine keycode(timeout)
$include int$keys.h
$include terminfo.h
$include keyin.h
$include keycode.h


   if kc.term.type # @term.type then   ;* Load bindings
      kc.keys = ''
      kc.codes = ''
      tinfo = terminfo()

* 19 Aug 26 Windows port - BOTH SPELLINGS OF BACKSPACE, BOUND BEFORE TERMINFO SO
*   TERMINFO STILL WINS.  A terminal sends either Ctrl-H (8) or DEL (127) for its
*   backspace key and there is no way to know which from the type alone.  SD took
*   whichever one kbs named and ignored the other, so the key did nothing at all
*   on a terminal that disagreed with its own terminfo entry.
*
*   MEASURED 19 Aug 2026: EVERY Windows console host - cmd, PowerShell and
*   Windows Terminal - sends DEL, while the default terminal type of the day was
*   vt100, whose kbs is ^H.  So backspace was dead out of the box on the platform
*   this port is for.  It is also why PuTTY had to be set to "Control-H": its
*   default is Control-?, which is DEL.
*
*   OF THE 62 TERMINFO ENTRIES HERE, 51 SAY ^H AND ONLY xterm AND linux SAY DEL,
*   so no choice of terminal type was going to fix it - which is what made
*   vt100-w look like it should have helped and not help.
*
*   LATER THE SAME DAY THE DEFAULT MOVED TO THE NEW windows TYPE, whose kbs is
*   DEL, so this bind is no longer what carries the key on a stock system - the
*   arrow keys were dead for the same reason and the type is what fixed those
*   (PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.18).  KEEP IT ANYWAY.  It costs two binds and it is
*   what makes the key survive a user typing TERM VT100, or an account whose
*   VOC still holds the old login paragraph, of which there will be many.
*
*   POSITION IS THE WHOLE POINT.  bind REPLACES an existing binding, so these two
*   are defaults that the terminfo binds below overwrite.  vt100-at (AccuTerm)
*   claims DEL for kdch1 and keeps it as the Delete key; only a type that leaves
*   a code unclaimed gets the fallback.  Additive in the same sense as the
*   three-case fold: it turns a lookup that finds nothing into one that finds
*   something, and changes no lookup that already succeeds.
*
*   THIS DOES NOT REACH ED.  UPDREC and SED read raw bytes with keyin() at
*   UPDREC:2171 and carry their own tables, where char(127) is bound to K$DELETE
*   (UPDREC:2416).  In the full-screen editors a DEL backspace still deletes
*   forwards.  Same family, separate fix.  PROJECT_STATUS.md 6.
      code = K$BACKSPACE  ; key.string = char(8)                ; gosub bind
      code = K$BACKSPACE  ; key.string = char(127)              ; gosub bind

* 02 Oct 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 120.  THE XTERM SPELLINGS OF F1-F5, HOME
*   AND END, BOUND THE SAME WAY AND FOR THE SAME REASON AS BACKSPACE ABOVE.
*   10 Nov 26: LINUX'S S.88 K1/K2 TABLE BELOW BINDS ALL SEVEN (ESC O P-S, ESC [ 15 ~ IN ITS F5-F12
*   LOOP, ESC [ H AND ESC [ F IN BOTH CURSOR-KEY MODES), SO THIS PORT'S OWN SEVEN LINES ARE GONE.
*
*   MEASURED 2 Oct 2026 with gplbld/probe-keys.ps1 in the owner's console (the
*   window was not named): F1-F4 send ESC O P, ESC O Q, ESC O R, ESC O S, F5 sends
*   ESC [ 1 5 ~, Home ESC [ H and End ESC [ F, while the arrows send ESC [ A-D and
*   Delete ESC [ 3 ~.  The windows type is a copy of linux and names ESC [ [ A-E
*   for F1-F5 and ESC [ 1 ~ / ESC [ 4 ~ for Home and End, so none of the seven
*   matched.  An unmatched ESC pair is thrown away with a bell below ("display
*   @sys.bell", key.string = ''), and the NEXT byte is returned as an ordinary
*   key: F1 typed a P, F2 a Q, F3 an R, F4 an S.  F1 therefore never reached
*   f1.help, which is how 108's message was found unreachable.
*
*   POSITION AGAIN IS THE POINT: bind REPLACES, so a terminfo type that claims
*   one of these strings for a different code still wins in the block below, and
*   one that claims it for the same code changes nothing.  What this adds is a
*   lookup that used to find nothing and now finds something.  F6-F12 are not
*   here because the windows type already binds the ESC [ nn ~ forms xterm sends
*   (not measured; the probe was asked for F1-F5, Home and End only).

* 28 Oct 26 dm - S.88 PHASE 1, K1/K2: THE KEYS OF EVERY TERMINAL IN BOTH CURSOR-KEY MODES, BOUND BEFORE TERMINFO SO TERMINFO STILL WINS.
*   The type in force was linux (LOGIN's paragraph forced it) or xterm, and a real xterm-family emulator (GNOME Terminal, Konsole, xterm) matches neither entry whole: in its default
*   normal mode it sends ESC [ A for Up and ESC O P for F1, while terminfo's linux entry has ESC [ [ A for F1 and its xterm entry has ESC O A for Up (the mode SD never switches on:
*   nothing sends smkx).  Measured on the owner's own GNOME Terminal 28 Oct 26: F1 arrived as alt-o then P, F2 as alt-o then Q, F5 as ~ and Home as nothing.  The strings below are what
*   xterm, VTE, Konsole, the Linux console, rxvt and a multiplexer send, in the normal and the application mode, and none of them is another key's string, so binding them all
*   changes no lookup that already succeeds.  smkx is NOT sent: its second half (ESC =, application keypad) would turn the numeric keypad into sequences nothing binds.
      for pm = 1 to 2
         pf = char(27) : '['
         if pm = 2 then pf = char(27) : 'O'
         code = K$UP    ; key.string = pf : 'A' ; gosub bind
         code = K$DOWN  ; key.string = pf : 'B' ; gosub bind
         code = K$RIGHT ; key.string = pf : 'C' ; gosub bind
         code = K$LEFT  ; key.string = pf : 'D' ; gosub bind
         code = K$HOME  ; key.string = pf : 'H' ; gosub bind
         code = K$END   ; key.string = pf : 'F' ; gosub bind
      next pm
      pf = char(27) : '['
      code = K$HOME      ; key.string = pf : '1~'  ; gosub bind
      code = K$HOME      ; key.string = pf : '7~'  ; gosub bind
      code = K$INSERT    ; key.string = pf : '2~'  ; gosub bind
      code = K$DELETE    ; key.string = pf : '3~'  ; gosub bind
      code = K$END       ; key.string = pf : '4~'  ; gosub bind
      code = K$END       ; key.string = pf : '8~'  ; gosub bind
      code = K$PAGE.UP   ; key.string = pf : '5~'  ; gosub bind
      code = K$PAGE.DOWN ; key.string = pf : '6~'  ; gosub bind
      code = K$CTRL.TAB  ; key.string = pf : 'Z'   ; gosub bind
*   F1 to F4: ESC O P, Q, R, S, and ESC [ 11~ to 14~
      code = K$F1 ; key.string = char(27) : 'OP' ; gosub bind
      code = K$F2 ; key.string = char(27) : 'OQ' ; gosub bind
      code = K$F3 ; key.string = char(27) : 'OR' ; gosub bind
      code = K$F4 ; key.string = char(27) : 'OS' ; gosub bind
*   F1 to F5 of the Linux console (ESC [ [ A to E): a console reached through a terminal of another type still has working keys
      code = K$F1 ; key.string = pf : '[A' ; gosub bind
      code = K$F2 ; key.string = pf : '[B' ; gosub bind
      code = K$F3 ; key.string = pf : '[C' ; gosub bind
      code = K$F4 ; key.string = pf : '[D' ; gosub bind
      code = K$F5 ; key.string = pf : '[E' ; gosub bind
      code = K$F1 ; key.string = pf : '11~' ; gosub bind
      code = K$F2 ; key.string = pf : '12~' ; gosub bind
      code = K$F3 ; key.string = pf : '13~' ; gosub bind
      code = K$F4 ; key.string = pf : '14~' ; gosub bind
      fkn = '15' : @fm : '17' : @fm : '18' : @fm : '19' : @fm : '20' : @fm : '21' : @fm : '23' : @fm : '24'
      for fk = 1 to 8
         code = K$F5 + fk - 1 ; key.string = pf : fkn<fk> : '~' ; gosub bind
      next fk
*   A bracketed paste (the screen library asks the terminal for it): ESC [ 200 ~ ... ESC [ 201 ~ around the pasted text.
      code = K$PASTE.START ; key.string = pf : '200~' ; gosub bind
      code = K$PASTE.END   ; key.string = pf : '201~' ; gosub bind

      code = K$F1        ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F1>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$F2         ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F2>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$F3         ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F3>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$F4         ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F4>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$F5         ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F5>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$F6         ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F6>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$F7         ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F7>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$F8         ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F8>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$F9         ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F9>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$F10        ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F10>       ; gosub bind
      code = K$F11        ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F11>       ; gosub bind
      code = K$F12        ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.F12>       ; gosub bind
      code = K$LEFT       ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.LEFT>      ; gosub bind
      code = K$RIGHT      ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.RIGHT>     ; gosub bind
      code = K$UP         ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.UP>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$DOWN       ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.DOWN>      ; gosub bind
      code = K$PAGE.UP    ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.PPAGE>     ; gosub bind
      code = K$PAGE.DOWN  ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.NPAGE>     ; gosub bind
      code = K$HOME       ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.HOME>      ; gosub bind
      code = K$END        ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.END>       ; gosub bind
      code = K$INSERT     ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.IC>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$DELETE     ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.DC>        ; gosub bind
      code = K$CTRL.TAB   ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.BTAB>      ; gosub bind
      code = K$MOUSE      ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.MOUSE>     ; gosub bind

* 18 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux 7ff476f (12 Oct 26): - AN XTERM SGR MOUSE REPORT (mode 1006: ESC [ < b ; x ; y M or m) IS A MOUSE KEY TOO.
*   Without a binding KEYCODE read ESC [ < , found no key that starts with it, rang the bell and
*   threw the three characters away, leaving "0;12;5M" in the input as if typed.  Now it returns
*   K$MOUSE and leaves b;x;y and the final M or m to be read (the screen library's $sl_key does).
*   Nothing changes for a program that never switches mouse reporting on.
      code = K$MOUSE      ; key.string = char(27) : '[<'        ; gosub bind
      code = K$BACKSPACE  ; key.string = tinfo<T$KEY.BACKSPACE> ; gosub bind
      code = K$RETURN     ; key.string = pterm(PT$INEWLINE,'')  ; gosub bind

      kc.term.type = @term.type
   end

   * A negative timeout value is used as a special case where we call this
   * subroutine simply to initialise the key bindings.

   if timeout < 0 then return value ''


   * Process input

   loop
      key.string = keyin(timeout)
      if key.string = '' then return value ''

      if key.string = char(27) then   ;* Leading Esc
         if not(kc.disable.esc) then
            key.string = keyin(0.2)
            set.status 0   ;* Hide possible timeout
            if key.string = '' then return value char(27)
            key.string = char(27) : key.string
         end
      end

      loop
         locate key.string in kc.keys<1> by 'AL' setting i then
            code = kc.codes<i>
            if code < 256 then       ;* Standard key definition
               return value char(code)
            end else                 ;* KEYEXIT/KEYTRAP definitions
               set.status code
               return value char(0)
            end
         end

      while kc.keys<i>[1,len(key.string)] = key.string
         c = keyin(timeout)
         if c = '' then return value ''
         key.string := c
      repeat

* 28 Oct 26 dm - S.88 PHASE 1, K3 AND K6: A SEQUENCE NOBODY BOUND IS TAKEN WHOLE, NOT CUT OFF.  ESC [ 1 ; 5 C (Ctrl+Right) read ESC [ 1 as the start of Home (ESC [ 1 ~), met the ;,
*   rang the bell and threw those four characters away - and the 5 and the C that were still to come were then TYPED.  A control sequence ends at its final byte (0x40 to 0x7E, after
*   parameter and intermediate bytes 0x20 to 0x3F), so the rest is read up to it.  For a program that asked for it (the screen library) the modifier is decoded: ESC [ 1 ; m X, ESC [ n ; m ~
*   come back as ESC, char(2) and the name (ctrl-right, shift-up, ctrl-pgup ...); a sequence with no name comes back as ESC, char(2) alone, which means "ignore it"; anyone else gets
*   the bell, as before, and still nothing typed.  An unbound ESC O x is whole at three characters and is ignored the same way.
      got = ''
      if key.string[1, 2] = char(27) : '[' and len(key.string) >= 3 then gosub csi.rest
      if key.string[1, 2] = char(27) : 'O' and len(key.string) = 3 and kc.alt then got = char(27) : char(2)
      if got # '' then return value got

      dropped = @false
      if len(key.string) = 1 then
* 28 Oct 26 dm - S.88 PHASE 1, K4: A BYTE OF 128 OR MORE IS NEVER A COMMAND.  An unbound single byte came back as itself, and $sl_key reads a character by its value, so a pasted UTF-8
*   bullet (E2 80 A2) was F1 (the IDE's help) and 0xC8 was the mouse key, which ended the IDE.  The keys that ARE bound return char(code) before this point and are not touched.  English
*   only: the byte is dropped, nothing is inserted.  Only for the screen library's read (kc.alt); a command line still gets what was typed.
         if kc.alt and seq(key.string) >= 128 then
            dropped = @true
         end else
            if system(1001) then key.string = swapcase(key.string)
            return value key.string
         end
      end

      * 22 Oct 26 dm - AN ALT-LETTER, FOR A PROGRAM THAT ASKED FOR IT (BINDKEY(, -7): the screen library's $sl_key does, for the one read).  A terminal
      *   sends Alt+x as Esc and x.  Nothing is bound to that, so KEYCODE rang the bell and threw the two characters away.  With kc.alt set it
      *   returns them, Esc first, for the caller to name.  A key that starts a bound sequence (Esc [ and Esc O) was read on above, so it is not this.
      * 31 Oct 26 dm - S.94, THE LINUX CONSOLE'S SHIFT-TAB: the kernel's default keymap sends Esc and Tab (measured on a real console, Debian 13), not ESC [ Z, so the back-tab of every form
      *   and dialogue was dropped there.  Only for the linux terminal type: for another type Esc and Tab is Alt-Tab, which is not this key.  (A block of its own, ahead of the Alt-letter's.)
      if kc.alt and key.string = char(27) : char(9) and downcase(@term.type) = 'linux' then return value char(K$CTRL.TAB)
      * 9 Nov 26 dm - S.184 stage 2: Esc and a blank (Alt-Space) and Esc and DEL or ^H (Alt-Backspace) come back too; $sl_key names them alt-  and alt-bs.
      if kc.alt and len(key.string) = 2 and key.string[1,1] = char(27) then
         alt.c = seq(key.string[2,1])
         if (alt.c >= 32 and alt.c <= 127) or alt.c = 8 then return value key.string
      end

      if not(dropped) then display @sys.bell :
      key.string = ''
   repeat

* ======================================================================
*  csi.rest  -  an unbound ESC [ sequence: read it to its final byte, and name it for the screen library (see above)

csi.rest:
   got = ''
   n = 0
   loop
      b = seq(key.string[len(key.string), 1])
   while b >= 32 and b <= 63 and n < 24 do
      c = keyin(0.2)
      set.status 0
      if c = '' then
         n = 99
      end else
         key.string := c
      end
      n += 1
   repeat
   if not(kc.alt) then return
   got = char(27) : char(2)
   fin = key.string[len(key.string), 1]
   pstr = key.string[3, len(key.string) - 3]
   base = ''
   p1 = field(pstr, ';', 1)
   md = field(pstr, ';', 2) + 0
   begin case
      case fin = 'A' ; base = 'up'
      case fin = 'B' ; base = 'down'
      case fin = 'C' ; base = 'right'
      case fin = 'D' ; base = 'left'
      case fin = 'H' ; base = 'home'
      case fin = 'F' ; base = 'end'
      case fin = 'P' ; base = 'f1'
      case fin = 'Q' ; base = 'f2'
      case fin = 'R' ; base = 'f3'
      case fin = 'S' ; base = 'f4'
      case fin = '~'
         begin case
            case p1 = 1 or p1 = 7 ; base = 'home'
            case p1 = 2 ; base = 'ins'
            case p1 = 3 ; base = 'del'
            case p1 = 4 or p1 = 8 ; base = 'end'
            case p1 = 5 ; base = 'pgup'
            case p1 = 6 ; base = 'pgdn'
            case p1 >= 11 and p1 <= 14 ; base = 'f' : (p1 - 10)
            case p1 = 15 ; base = 'f5'
            case p1 = 17 ; base = 'f6'
            case p1 = 18 ; base = 'f7'
            case p1 = 19 ; base = 'f8'
            case p1 = 20 ; base = 'f9'
            case p1 = 21 ; base = 'f10'
            case p1 = 23 ; base = 'f11'
            case p1 = 24 ; base = 'f12'
         end case
   end case
   if base # '' and md >= 2 and md <= 8 then
      pre = ''
      if bitand(md - 1, 4) then pre := 'ctrl-'
      if bitand(md - 1, 2) then pre := 'alt-'
      if bitand(md - 1, 1) then pre := 'shift-'
      got = char(27) : char(2) : pre : base
   end
   * 31 Oct 26 dm - S.94, THE LINUX CONSOLE'S SHIFT-F1 TO SHIFT-F8: the kernel's default keymap sends them as F13 to F20 (ESC [ 25 ~, 26, 28, 29, 31, 32, 33, 34 ~; measured on a real
   *   console, Debian 13), with no modifier, so Shift-F6 (the IDE's previous pane) and Shift-F3 (the editor's find previous) named nothing there.  Only for the linux terminal type: rxvt
   *   and the VT220 numbering give those strings other meanings.  Shift-F9 to Shift-F12 and Ctrl-F1 to Ctrl-F12 send nothing on the console and cannot be helped here.
   if fin = '~' and md < 2 and base = '' and downcase(@term.type) = 'linux' then
      sfn = 0
      begin case
         case p1 = 25 ; sfn = 1
         case p1 = 26 ; sfn = 2
         case p1 = 28 ; sfn = 3
         case p1 = 29 ; sfn = 4
         case p1 = 31 ; sfn = 5
         case p1 = 32 ; sfn = 6
         case p1 = 33 ; sfn = 7
         case p1 = 34 ; sfn = 8
      end case
      if sfn then got = char(27) : char(2) : 'shift-f' : sfn
   end
   return


* ======================================================================
*  bind  -  Set a binding

bind:
   if len(key.string) then
      locate key.string in kc.keys<1> by 'AL' setting i then ;* Replace binding
         kc.codes<i> = code
      end else                                               ;* Add binding
         ins key.string before kc.keys<i>
         ins code before kc.codes<i>
      end
   end

   return
end

* END-CODE
